\section*{Combinatorial Invariance of Kazhdan–Lusztig Polynomials}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Kazhdan--Lusztig invariance}: The manuscript claims equality of equal-parameter Kazhdan--Lusztig polynomials whenever two Bruhat intervals are isomorphic as posets. The intervals may lie in unrelated Coxeter systems.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Kazhdan--Lusztig invariance}: Moment-graph sheaves encode the polynomials as graded ranks of vertex modules. A reflection order from the second interval supplies a transported edge schedule on the first. Induction proves both polynomial equality and freeness of kernels for terminal edge sets; the local edge step makes the sheaf calculation independent of the original labels.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Kazhdan--Lusztig invariance}: A directed Bruhat-graph isomorphism preserves reachability and hence the Bruhat-interval order. The claimed poset-isomorphism theorem uses strictly less data and applies to all Coxeter systems.

The claimed result implies the stated question and also supplies the additional strength described above.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 168 describes Lean coverage for preservation of equal-parameter polynomials under Bruhat-poset isomorphisms across Coxeter systems.

\textit{Kazhdan--Lusztig invariance}: The manuscript links \texttt{KLInvariance.lean}.

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

